# Crystal mutation operators for universalmutator's regex generator.
#
# This file is a TRANSLITERATION, not a design. Every operator class below is
# carried over from a rules file universalmutator ships; the Crystal column is
# only a change of syntax. That is deliberate: a 100% mutation score against
# operators we invented ourselves would attest to less, because the same blind
# spot that shaped the code would have shaped the operators. Adding or removing
# an operator class is therefore a reviewed change, not an implementation
# choice — and every shipped rules file is accounted for below, either by a
# SOURCE block that carries it across or by one that records why Crystal has no
# counterpart. `tests/mutate_rules.bats` enforces that accounting.
#
# Format: `<python regex>`, then a literal arrow, then `<replacement>`,
# applied line by line. Two
# replacements are sentinels: DO_NOT_MUTATE skips the whole line, and
# SKIP_MUTATING_REST skips from the match position to end of line.
#
# The runner (scripts/mutate.sh) passes this file with `--only`, so these rules
# are the entire operator set — universal.rules is NOT also loaded.

DO_NOT_MUTATE ==> DO_NOT_MUTATE

# SOURCE: c_like.rules, java.rules, python.rules, r.rules, solidity.rules
# Lines that carry no behaviour. `#include`/`import` become `require`; the
# `//` and `#` comment skips become Crystal's `#`, with a lookahead so string
# interpolation (`#{...}`) is not mistaken for a comment. Decorator/annotation
# lines (java's `@.+`) are Crystal's `@[Annotation]`.
(^\s*require\s) ==> DO_NOT_MUTATE
(^\s*#) ==> DO_NOT_MUTATE
(^\s*@\[) ==> DO_NOT_MUTATE
#(?!\{) ==> SKIP_MUTATING_REST

# SOURCE: universal.rules — arithmetic operator replacement.
# Crystal adds `//` (floor division) and `**` (exponent) to the C set; both are
# folded in here rather than given a class of their own, since fortran.rules
# already ships `**` as an arithmetic operator.
\+ ==> -
\+ ==> *
\+ ==> /
\+ ==> %
\+ ==> **
-([^>]) ==> +\1
-([^>]) ==> *\1
-([^>]) ==> /\1
-([^>]) ==> %\1
-([^>]) ==> **\1
\*\* ==> +
\*\* ==> -
\*\* ==> *
\*\* ==> /
([^\*])\*([^\*]) ==> \1+\2
([^\*])\*([^\*]) ==> \1-\2
([^\*])\*([^\*]) ==> \1/\2
([^\*])\*([^\*]) ==> \1%\2
([^\*/])/([^\*/]) ==> \1+\2
([^\*/])/([^\*/]) ==> \1-\2
([^\*/])/([^\*/]) ==> \1*\2
([^\*/])/([^\*/]) ==> \1%\2
([^\*/])/([^\*/]) ==> \1//\2
// ==> /
% ==> +
% ==> -
% ==> *
% ==> /

# SOURCE: universal.rules — unary-minus deletion. The `[^>]` guard keeps `->`
# (a proc literal in Crystal, as it is an arrow elsewhere) intact.
-([^>]) ==> \1

# SOURCE: universal.rules — assignment-operator replacement. `x += 1` becoming
# `x =+ 1` is `x = +1`: the accumulate is dropped but the line still parses,
# because Crystal has unary `+` and `-` like C does.
\+= ==> =+
-= ==> =-

# SOURCE: universal.rules — increment/decrement swap (`++`/`--`).
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: Crystal has no increment or decrement operator; `x++`
# is a parse error, so every mutant this class could produce is uncompilable.

# SOURCE: universal.rules — relational operator replacement. The guards are
# wider than the shipped ones because Crystal spells more things with angle
# brackets: `=>` (hash and rescue), `->` (proc), `<<` and `>>` (shift and
# heredoc), `<=>` (comparison), and `class Foo < Bar` (inheritance).
!= ==> ==
!= ==> <=
!= ==> >=
!= ==> >
!= ==> <
([^!<>=])==([^=]) ==> \1!=\2
([^!<>=])==([^=]) ==> \1<=\2
([^!<>=])==([^=]) ==> \1>=\2
([^!<>=])==([^=]) ==> \1>\2
([^!<>=])==([^=]) ==> \1<\2
([^<])>=([^=]) ==> \1==\2
([^<])>=([^=]) ==> \1!=\2
([^<])>=([^=]) ==> \1<\2
([^<])>=([^=]) ==> \1>\2
<=([^>]) ==> ==\1
<=([^>]) ==> !=\1
<=([^>]) ==> <\1
<=([^>]) ==> >\1
([^<=])<([^<=]) ==> \1>\2
([^<=])<([^<=]) ==> \1==\2
([^<=])<([^<=]) ==> \1<=\2
([^<=])<([^<=]) ==> \1>=\2
([^<=])<([^<=]) ==> \1!=\2
([^-=<>])>([^=>]) ==> \1<\2
([^-=<>])>([^=>]) ==> \1==\2
([^-=<>])>([^=>]) ==> \1>=\2
([^-=<>])>([^=>]) ==> \1<=\2
([^-=<>])>([^=>]) ==> \1!=\2

# SOURCE: javascript.rules — strict-equality swap (`===` to `!==`).
# Crystal spells case-equality `===` and has no `!==`, so the counterpart is a
# demotion to `==`, which is what `!==` is in a language that lacks it.
=== ==> ==

# SOURCE: universal.rules — numeric literal replacement.
(\D)(\d+)(\D) ==> \g<1>0\3
(\D)(\d+)(\D) ==> \g<1>1\3
(\D)(\d+)(\D) ==> \g<1>-1\3
(\D)(\d+)(\D) ==> \1(\2+1)\3
(\D)(\d+)(\D) ==> \1(\2-1)\3

# SOURCE: universal.rules, lisp.rules, python.rules — logical operator
# replacement and negation deletion. Crystal has no `and`/`or` keyword forms,
# so lisp's and python's word operators collapse onto the symbol forms.
&& ==> ||
\|\| ==> &&
! ==>

# SOURCE: universal.rules — bitwise operator replacement.
([^&])&([^&]) ==> \1|\2
([^|])\|([^|]) ==> \1&\2

# SOURCE: go.rules, java.rules, javascript.rules, swift.rules — short-circuit
# operand forcing. Identical in Crystal; `true`/`false` are spelled the same.
&& .* ==> && true
\|\| .* ==> || false
^(\s*).* && ==> \1true &&
^(\s*).* \|\| ==> \1false ||

# SOURCE: rust.rules, swift.rules, python.rules, lisp.rules — boolean literal
# swap. lisp's `T`/`nil` pair is the same class in a language with `true`.
true ==> false
false ==> true

# SOURCE: c_like.rules, python.rules, solidity.rules — conditional negation and
# forcing. Crystal has no parentheses around a condition and adds the inverted
# keywords `unless` and `until`, so each shipped form gets a keyword-led
# Crystal counterpart covering both the block and the modifier position.
(^\s*)if (.+)\n ==> \1if !(\2)\n
(^\s*)if (.+)\n ==> \1if true\n
(^\s*)if (.+)\n ==> \1if false\n
(^\s*)unless (.+)\n ==> \1unless !(\2)\n
(^\s*)unless (.+)\n ==> \1unless true\n
(^\s*)unless (.+)\n ==> \1unless false\n
(^\s*)while (.+)\n ==> \1while !(\2)\n
(^\s*)until (.+)\n ==> \1until !(\2)\n
\bif (.+)\n ==> if !(\1)\n
\bunless (.+)\n ==> unless !(\1)\n
\bif ==> unless
\bunless ==> if
\bwhile ==> until
\buntil ==> while

# SOURCE: universal.rules — loop-to-conditional weakening (`while` to `if`).
\bwhile ==> if
\buntil ==> if

# SOURCE: c_like.rules — `else` deletion, which folds the alternative branch
# into the consequent.
(^\s*)else\n ==> \1
(^\s*)else$ ==> \1

# SOURCE: universal.rules — loop-control statement insertion. C's `break;` and
# `continue;` lose the semicolon, and `continue` is spelled `next`.
(^\s*)(\S+.*)\n ==> \1\2\n\1break\n
(^\s*)(\S+.*)\n ==> \1\2\n\1next\n

# SOURCE: python.rules — `continue`/`break` swap.
\bcontinue\b ==> break
\bbreak\b ==> next
\bnext\b ==> break

# SOURCE: python.rules, c_like.rules — statement deletion. python replaces the
# line with `pass`; c_like comments it out. Crystal has no `pass`, so the
# comment form is the one that carries over. The lookahead excludes lines that
# open or close a block: deleting one of those can only ever be a parse error,
# and each rejected mutant costs a compile.
(^\s*)(?!(?:end|else|elsif|when|in|rescue|ensure|begin|module|class|struct|enum|def|macro|lib|union|annotation|abstract|private|protected|case|loop)\b)(?!.*\bdo\b\s*(?:\|[^|]*\|)?\s*$)(?!.*\bthen$)(\S.*)\n ==> \1# \2\n

# SOURCE: python.rules — `return` value replacement. python returns `None`;
# Crystal returns `nil`. Paired with statement deletion above, this is what
# strips an early-return guard.
(^\s*)return\b.*\n ==> \1return nil\n

# SOURCE: universal.rules — string literal emptying.
".+" ==> ""

# SOURCE: python.rules, swift.rules — collection literal emptying and element
# dropping, for `[...]` only.
\[.+\] ==> [] of typeof(nil)
\[.*, ==> [
,.*\] ==> ]
,.+, ==> ,

# SOURCE: python.rules, swift.rules — `{...}` collection emptying.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: `{}` is not an empty Hash or Tuple in Crystal (`{} of
# K => V` is required), and `{...}` is also the short block form, so the shipped
# regexes would mostly rewrite blocks into parse errors rather than into empty
# collections.

# SOURCE: python.rules — single-quoted string emptying (`'.+'` to `''`).
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: `'x'` is a Char literal in Crystal and `''` is a parse
# error, so this class has no compilable Crystal form.

# SOURCE: universal.rules — argument order swapping.
([,\(\[\{])([^=,\)\}\]]+),([^=,\)\}\]]+)([,\)\]\}]) ==>  \1\3,\2\4
([,\(\[\{])([^,=\)\]\}]+)=([^,=\)\]\}]+),([^,=\)\]\}]+)=([^,=\)\]\}]+)([,\)\]\}]) ==> \1\4=\3,\2=\5\6
([,\(\[\{])([^,:\)\]\}]+): ([^,\)\]\}]+),([^,:\)\]\}]+): ([^,\)\]\}]+)([,\)\]\}]) ==> \1\4: \3,\2: \5\6

# SOURCE: cpp.rules — library-call substitution: swapping a call for its
# nearest neighbour in the same family. Crystal's Enumerable spells the same
# families with `?`-suffixed predicates and `_by` selectors.
\bany\? ==> all?
\bany\? ==> none?
\ball\? ==> any?
\ball\? ==> none?
\bnone\? ==> any?
\bnone\? ==> all?
\bmin_by\b ==> max_by
\bmax_by\b ==> min_by
\bunshift\b ==> push
\bpush\b ==> unshift
\bsum\b ==> product
\bproduct\b ==> sum
\.at\(.*\) ==> .at(0)

# SOURCE: universal.rules — `min`/`max` swap.
\bmin\b ==> max
\bmax\b ==> min

# SOURCE: universal.rules — `begin`/`end` swap.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: `begin` and `end` delimit blocks in Crystal rather
# than naming positions the way the shipped rule's C-family targets use them.
# Swapping them can only unbalance the parse, never produce a program that
# compiles and behaves differently, so the class would spend one compile per
# line of every file for no reachable mutant.

# SOURCE: java.rules — `synchronized` removal.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: Crystal has no `synchronized` keyword; mutual
# exclusion is a library type (`Mutex`), not a modifier on a declaration.

# SOURCE: go.rules — `defer` removal.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: Crystal has no `defer`; the equivalent is an
# `ensure` block, which the statement-deletion class above already reaches.

# SOURCE: swift.rules — `var` to `let`.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: Crystal has no mutability keyword on a local binding.

# SOURCE: solidity.rules, vyper.rules, fe.rules, tact.rules, func.rules,
# tolk.rules, ton_common.rules — contract-language operators (visibility and
# mutability modifiers, `msg.sender`/`tx.origin`, ether and time units,
# storage classes, `selfdestruct`/`revert` insertion).
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: these name constructs of the EVM and TON contract
# languages that have no general-purpose Crystal equivalent.

# SOURCE: fortran.rules — `.lt.`/`.LT.` style relational and logical operators.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART: this is fortran's spelling of the relational and
# logical classes already carried above in their symbol form.

# SOURCE: lisp.rules — `T`/`nil` swap and word-form `and`/`or`/`not`.
# NO CRYSTAL COUNTERPART BEYOND THE ABOVE: carried by the boolean-literal and
# logical-operator classes; Crystal has no word-form logical operators.

# SOURCE: r.rules — `#` comment skip. Carried in the DO_NOT_MUTATE block above.

# SOURCE: c.rules, none.rules, vyper.rules, fe.rules — all four ship empty.
# `vyper` and `fe` compose python.rules and solidity.rules instead of adding
# operators of their own, and both of those are accounted for above.
